\title{Dot product of two \code{\verb~|Vec<f32>~} with a vector accumulator}
\taxon{Example}
\date{2026-11-13}
\author{claude-fable-5-2}
\contributor{schrodingerzy}
\tag{simd}
\tag{example}
\import{base-macros}

\p{The accumulator is a scalable vector carried as loop state. This is not accepted yet: see [prob-I2N9](prob-I2N9). With a fixed vector type the example is within the rules. The reduction happens once, after the loop.}
\pre{\verb~|
import core::ptr;
import core::simd;
import core::ptr::PtrArithFlag;
import std::collections::cow::vec::Vec;

pub fn dot(a: Vec<f32>, b: Vec<f32>) -> f32 {
    let len = if a.len() < b.len() { a.len() } else { b.len() };
    core::object::unsafe_inc(a);
    core::object::unsafe_inc(b);
    let pa = a.unsafe_data_ptr();
    let pb = b.unsafe_data_ptr();
    let width = simd::lanes<Simd<[f32; [4]]>>();
    let zero = simd::splat<Simd<[f32; [4]]>>(0.0);
    let (end, acc) = while [i = 0, acc = zero] (i < len) {
        let live = simd::mask_first<Simd<[bool; [4]]>>(len - i);
        let x = simd::load<Simd<[f32; [4]]>>(ptr::advance<f32>(pa, i as i64, PtrArithFlag::Inbounds), live, zero);
        let y = simd::load<Simd<[f32; [4]]>>(ptr::advance<f32>(pb, i as i64, PtrArithFlag::Inbounds), live, zero);
        (i + width, simd::fma(x, y, acc, live))
    };
    core::object::unsafe_dec(a);
    core::object::unsafe_dec(b);
    simd::reduce_add(acc)
}
~}
\ul{
\li{\code{\verb~|fma(x, y, acc, live)~} computes \code{\verb~|x * y + acc~}. Its masked lanes keep the first operand, so the argument order that keeps the accumulator in the tail has to be settled when \code{\verb~|fma~} is specified; passing zeros as the pass-through of both loads makes the tail lanes add zero either way.}
\li{The lanes are summed in lane order. Allowing reassociation in \code{\verb~|reduce_add~} is what lets the target use its fastest reduction.}
}
\p{Belongs to [SIMD vectors](design-simd-vectors). A fixed-vector form of it runs in the standard library tests, \pr{1006}.}
